WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.000
Delegate clearly, keep a healthy pace, and review every week.

2
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:09.430
This is the video for Module 3 of Productivity and Time Management at Work.

3
00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:20.450
A good system must work week after week without wearing you out. Delegation means handing a task to someone else while you stay responsible for the result.

4
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.290
Then you learn to protect your pace, and to run a weekly review that shows whether your methods work.

5
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:34.700
Delegate tasks that repeat and that someone else can learn. Keep decisions that need your authority.

6
00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:41.560
Then write a brief. First, the outcome. A reorder list in the shared sheet is clearer than, check the stock.

7
00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:44.980
Give a deadline, with a date and time.

8
00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:50.090
Share the resources: files, templates, or past examples.

9
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:56.570
Say what they can decide alone, and what needs your approval. Move them up as trust grows.

10
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.830
And agree when you will check in, so it feels normal, not like suspicion.

11
00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:11.980
Most failed delegation is a briefing problem, so put it in writing. And never delegate access to your bank app, PINs or passwords.

12
00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:24.710
Esther spends about 3 hours every Saturday on stock. She writes Kunle a brief on WhatsApp, with the outcome, a deadline, the sheet template, and the rule that she approves and pays.

13
00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:34.980
Week 1 they count together. Then Kunle counts alone, and Esther checks 10 items at random. Two errors in week 2, none in week 3.

14
00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:45.130
In week 4 he recommends, and she decides. She saves about 2.5 hours a week for building relationships with two nearby schools.

15
00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:57.040
The World Health Organization describes burn-out as resulting from chronic workplace stress that has not been managed. It has three dimensions. First, exhaustion.

16
00:01:58.290 --> 00:02:02.540
Second, mental distance from your job, or feeling negative about it.

17
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:10.640
Third, feeling less effective. And here is the trap. Working more hours to catch up often feeds the cycle.

18
00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:18.540
End each workday with 10 minutes to capture loose ends, check tomorrow, and choose the first task. Then stop.

19
00:02:19.790 --> 00:02:26.680
When a new request arrives and your week is full, ask which task should give way, rather than silently adding hours.

20
00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:30.880
Take a real lunch break, and plan your leave early.

21
00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:36.710
Sleep, regular meals, water and some movement are what attention runs on.

22
00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:54.560
If exhaustion, low mood, poor sleep or loss of interest continue for weeks, speak to a doctor or a qualified counsellor, and to HR if you have one. If you ever feel unable to cope, contact a health professional immediately and tell someone you trust.

23
00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:01.700
Adaeze completed only 2 of her 5 priorities last week, and she feels drained every morning.

24
00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:10.140
She lists her commitments with hours. 46 hours of work, against about 32 hours of real capacity.

25
00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:21.050
She brings options, not complaints. I suggest these two changes. Which would you prefer? They delegate reconciliations and move one audit deadline.

26
00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.840
Within three weeks she leaves by 6 most days, and completes 4 of 5 priorities.

27
00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:37.520
Systems decay. So block a fixed weekly slot, like Friday afternoon. First, get clear. Empty your capture inbox and saved messages.

28
00:03:38.770 --> 00:03:45.280
Get current. Tick off finished actions, follow up anything overdue on Waiting for, and check your calendar.

29
00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:51.370
Get creative. Look at your OKR. Is there a new idea or risk to act on?

30
00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:57.970
Then choose next week's top three priorities, and block time for them before the week fills up.

31
00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:10.530
Each week she records priorities finished, focused hours, interruptions, and her energy from 1 to 5. And she changes only one thing each week, so she can see what helped.

32
00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:20.850
Week 1, just 6 focused hours. She had planned three large reports, so she broke them into sections. By week 4, 10 focused hours.

33
00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:29.780
Interruptions fell from 15 to 7 a day after she muted the staff group chat. Now she has numbers to show her manager.

34
00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:42.700
To remember. Delegate with a written brief, and track it on Waiting for. More hours often feeds burnout, so use a shutdown routine. Review every week, and change one thing at a time.

35
00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:54.760
Now your turn. Delegate one real task with a written brief, build your sustainable-pace plan with a shutdown routine, and run your first weekly review with a scorecard.

36
00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:57.050
Continue with the lessons below.
